1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,752
The Budapest gambit gives up a pawn to accelerate every piece.

2
00:00:04,912 --> 00:00:09,365
Black develops the knight before chasing the pawn.

3
00:00:09,525 --> 00:00:13,977
White takes, and the knight jumps into the center.

4
00:00:14,137 --> 00:00:18,590
The second knight joins with pressure on the e pawn.

5
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:23,203
White spends a move asking the bishop a question.

6
00:00:23,363 --> 00:00:27,815
Black pushes d six and prepares to recover the pawn.

7
00:00:27,975 --> 00:00:32,428
The pawn takes again, but the bishop recaptures with tempo.

8
00:00:32,587 --> 00:00:37,040
Both sides build toward a sharp middlegame.

9
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,652
Black castles and the queen comes to f six.

10
00:00:41,812 --> 00:00:46,265
The pressure is compensation, not a promise of free material.

11
00:00:46,425 --> 00:00:50,877
The lesson: make every developing move attack something.

12
00:00:51,037 --> 00:00:55,650
Would you play the Budapest gambit?

